      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Advocates often invoke concepts like divergent selective pressures related to ancestral environments, suggesting that different geographic or ecological niches favored distinct cognitive adaptations over millennia. They might reference population bottlenecks or founder effects to theorize that genetic drift amplified certain traits within isolated groups. Some theories propose specific evolutionary trade-offs, suggesting that traits beneficial in one ecological context might inherently constrain cognitive development in another. These mechanisms are typically used to provide a purported naturalistic justification for observed group differences, though the scientific consensus rejects these causal links.",
      "source": "target",
